APX.sk = {"country":{"name":"Slovakia","tagline":"Little Carpathian royal towns, Danube plain reds and a slice of Tokaj that both sides of the border claim.","classification":"Slovak wine follows EU categories. Five regional PDOs (Malokarpatská, Južnoslovenská, Nitrianska, Stredoslovenská, Východoslovenská) and the Vinohradnícka oblasť Tokaj PDO are registered, each with grammatical variants; two more local PDOs are Karpatská perla (2011, a quality label for the Bratislava and Little Carpathian area) and Skalický rubín (2017, a red blend from Skalica). Quality wines with a predicate follow the German ladder (kabinetné, neskorý zber, výber z hrozna, bobuľový výber, hrozienkový výber, cibébový výber, ľadové víno, slamové víno). Special terms include mladé víno (young wine sold from the first Monday in November), archívne víno (aged three years) and panenská úroda (first harvest of a new vineyard)."},"regions":[{"name":"Malokarpatská","slug":"malokarpatsk","tagline":"Royal wine towns on the slopes above Bratislava.","ids":["sk-karpatska-perla","sk-malokarpatska","sk-skalicky-rubin"]},{"name":"Južnoslovenská","slug":"ju-noslovensk","tagline":"The warm Danube plain, rich whites and reds.","ids":["sk-juznoslovenska"]},{"name":"Nitrianska","slug":"nitrianska","tagline":"Loess hills around the old capital of Nitra.","ids":["sk-nitrianska"]},{"name":"Stredoslovenská","slug":"stredoslovensk","tagline":"Small vineyards of the southern central hills.","ids":["sk-stredoslovenska"]},{"name":"Východoslovenská","slug":"v-chodoslovensk","tagline":"Volcanic hills of the east, next to Tokaj.","ids":["sk-vychodoslovenska"]},{"name":"Tokaj (Slovakia)","slug":"tokaj-slovakia","tagline":"Seven villages of volcanic Tokaj on the Slovak side of the border.","ids":["sk-vinohradnicka-oblast-tokaj"]}],"items":{"sk-juznoslovenska":{"name":"Južnoslovenská / Južnoslovenské / Južnoslovenský","cat":"PDO","region":"Južnoslovenská","created":"2006 (EU reg.)","styles":["white","red","rose","sparkling","sweet"],"grapes":"Grüner Veltliner, Welschriesling, Blaufränkisch, Müller-Thurgau, Saint Laurent, Riesling, Pinot Blanc, Devín and others","summary":"The South Slovak PDO (EU reg. 2006) for the Danube plain, 120 municipalities from Šamorín to Štúrovo. Warm, dry and sandy, it gives ripe whites and reds; Strekov and Belá have become sources of some of the country's most talked-about wines.","producers":["Château Belá","Strekov 1075","Vinárstvo Chowaniec & Krajčírovič","Vinárske závody Topoľčianky"],"nerd_note":"Château Belá's Riesling, made with Egon Müller of the Saar, put Slovakia on serious wine lists from the early 2000s.","rslug":"ju-noslovensk"},"sk-karpatska-perla":{"name":"Karpatská perla","cat":"PDO","region":"Malokarpatská","created":"2011 (EU reg.)","styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Many varieties","summary":"Karpatská perla ('Carpathian pearl') is a PDO registered in 2011 for 34 municipalities of Bratislava and the Little Carpathian foothills, used as a tasting-controlled quality label for local wines. Not to be confused with the producer Karpatská Perla in Šenkvice.","terroir":"Granite and loess of the Little Carpathian foothills.","rslug":"malokarpatsk"},"sk-malokarpatska":{"name":"Malokarpatská / Malokarpatské / Malokarpatský","cat":"PDO","region":"Malokarpatská","created":"2006 (EU reg.)","styles":["white","red","rose","sparkling","sweet"],"grapes":"Grüner Veltliner, Welschriesling, Blaufränkisch, Müller-Thurgau, Saint Laurent, Riesling, Pinot Blanc, Devín and others","summary":"The Little Carpathian PDO (EU reg. 2006), covering 143 municipalities from Bratislava to Skalica. It includes the wine towns of Rača, Svätý Jur, Pezinok and Modra, whose burghers made the region's fortune, and modern producers who have revived Grüner Veltliner, Riesling and Frankovka from granite slopes.","producers":["Víno Matyšák","Mrva & Stanko","Karpatská Perla","Víno Masaryk","Víno Natural Domin & Kušický","Strekov 1075"],"nerd_note":"Rača in Bratislava has been famous for Frankovka (Blaufränkisch) for centuries; its annual harvest festival celebrates the grape.","rslug":"malokarpatsk"},"sk-nitrianska":{"name":"Nitrianska / Nitrianske / Nitriansky","cat":"PDO","region":"Nitrianska","created":"2006 (EU reg.)","styles":["white","red","rose","sparkling","sweet"],"grapes":"Grüner Veltliner, Welschriesling, Blaufränkisch, Müller-Thurgau, Saint Laurent, Riesling, Pinot Blanc, Devín and others","summary":"The Nitra PDO (EU reg. 2006), the largest Slovak region by municipalities (192), around Nitra, Hlohovec, Zlaté Moravce and Vráble. Loess hills give balanced whites and reds; Château Topoľčianky is a key producer.","producers":["Château Topoľčianky","Vinárstvo Pomfy-Mavín","Víno Nitra"],"rslug":"nitrianska"},"sk-skalicky-rubin":{"name":"Skalický rubín","cat":"PDO","region":"Malokarpatská","created":"2017 (EU reg.)","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Blaufränkisch, Blauer Portugieser, Saint Laurent","summary":"Skalický rubín ('Skalica ruby') is a traditional red blend from six municipalities around Skalica in the far west of Slovakia, near the Moravian border, registered as a PDO in 2017. It must blend Frankovka modrá, Modrý Portugal and Svätovavrinecké, revived from a nineteenth-century local tradition.","terroir":"Loess and sand at the foot of the White Carpathians.","nerd_note":"One of the few PDOs defined by a specific red blend of three grapes.","rslug":"malokarpatsk"},"sk-stredoslovenska":{"name":"Stredoslovenská / Stredoslovenský / Stredoslovenské","cat":"PDO","region":"Stredoslovenská","created":"2006 (EU reg.)","styles":["white","red","rose","sparkling","sweet"],"grapes":"Grüner Veltliner, Welschriesling, Blaufränkisch, Müller-Thurgau, Saint Laurent, Riesling, Pinot Blanc, Devín and others","summary":"The Central Slovak PDO (EU reg. 2006), 133 municipalities along the southern border around Modrý Kameň, Fiľakovo and Rimavská Sobota, with small family vineyards.","rslug":"stredoslovensk"},"sk-vinohradnicka-oblast-tokaj":{"name":"Vinohradnícka oblasť Tokaj","cat":"PDO","region":"Tokaj (Slovakia)","created":"2006 (EU reg.); upheld by EU courts 2012 and 2013","area_ha":908,"styles":["sweet","white"],"grapes":"Furmint, Lipovina, Muškát žltý","summary":"The Slovak Tokaj PDO covers seven villages and about 908 hectares of classified vineyards on the northern edge of the Zemplén hills. It makes the full range of Tokaj styles: dry Furmint and Lipovina, samorodné from bunches with botrytised berries, Tokajský výber (the Slovak name for aszú) graded from 3 to 6 puttony, výberová esencia and esencia, plus mášláš and fordítáš made by re-fermenting must on aszú lees. The Hungarian state long argued that only Hungarian wine should bear the name, but after the 2004 bilateral agreement and failed EU court actions in 2012 and 2013, both countries use it. Malá Tŕňa and Veľká Tŕňa, with tuff cellars dating back centuries, are the centre.","terroir":"Volcanic tuff, andesite and loess; humid autumns from the Bodrog river favour botrytis.","producers":["Tokaj Macik Winery","J&J Ostrožovič","Zlatý strapec","Tokaj & Co.","Čerhov Tokaj Winery"],"nerd_note":"Slovak Tokaj producers still nominally follow the Hungarian Tokaj law of 1908 (Law XLVII) for traditional styles.","rslug":"tokaj-slovakia"},"sk-vychodoslovenska":{"name":"Východoslovenská / Východoslovenský / Východoslovenské","cat":"PDO","region":"Východoslovenská","created":"2006 (EU reg.)","styles":["white","red","rose","sparkling","sweet"],"grapes":"Grüner Veltliner, Welschriesling, Blaufränkisch, Müller-Thurgau, Saint Laurent, Riesling, Pinot Blanc, Devín and others","summary":"The East Slovak PDO (EU reg. 2006), 99 municipalities around Košice, Moldava nad Bodvou and the Zemplín outside the Tokaj delimitation. Volcanic and loess soils near the Tokaj hills give aromatic whites, including Furmint and Lipovina sold without the Tokaj name.","rslug":"v-chodoslovensk"}}};
